  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to allowing two automatic free transfers for riders using pay-per-ride Metrocards",
    "basePrintNo": "S2560",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2560-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1266, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2560\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to allowing two automatic free transfers for riders using\npay-per-ride Metrocards\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo ensure that riders of the Metropolitan Transportation Authority\nmass-transit system do not incur extra costs due to recent cuts in\nservice.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill amends section 1266 of the Public Authorities\nLaw by adding a new subdivision (3-b) that requires the Metropolitan\nTransportation Authority (MTA) to automatically apply two free\ntransfers to each paid fare or rate and be valid for two hours from\nthe time the fare is purchased. These transfers can used to transfer\nbetween subways and the buses that are owned and operated by the MTA\nas well as transfers between the MTA's buses. This transfer policy may\nbe amended from time to time.\n\nSection two of this bill provides that this act shall take effect\nimmediately.\n\n CURRENT LAW :\n\nCurrently, holders of unlimited ride MetroCards have unlimited\ntransfers. Holders of pay-per-ride MetroCards receive only 1 free\ntransfer.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, upon purchasing a pay-per-ride MetroCard, the rider is\nallowed one automatic free transfer per fare. Unfortunately, due to\nrecent cutbacks in services and the outright elimination of various\nbus and subway lines, some riders now must purchase two fares to make\ntheir daily commute. Essentially the MTA has imposed new fares on\nriders who have enough trouble paying for one fare. The avoidable\nburden caused by these cutbacks, which is the additional financial\nburden, should not be passed on to riders in these affected areas.\nThis bill would ensure that no commuter or rider financially suffers\ndue to cutbacks in service that already add time and stress to a\ncommute.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.6484 - Referred to Transportation\n2015: S.3228 - Vetoed, Memo 254\n2014: S.2092-A - Passed the Senate\n2013: S.2092 - Referred to Transportation\n2011-12: S.3839- Referred to Transportation\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
